  "cache_policy": {
    "cached": "parsed panels and the maps beside them -- the .npz markout files and the per-wallet taker maps. They are what every analysis node reads, they are deterministic given the tape, and they are what a rebuild is trying to avoid.",
    "raw_days": "the raw tape, deleted by default. Each day arrives as 24 hourly compressed files and is parsed; whether the day's directory then survives is decided by CAP_KEEP_RAW, and CAP_RAW_CACHE says where it lives. A day already in that directory with a full complement of hourly files is reused instead of refetched. No build node stands for a raw day either way, kept or deleted, so nothing in this graph can ask for one directly.",
    "why": "the system volume cannot hold thirty days of the tape at once, so deleting is what a long unattended run wants and is the default. Writing the rule here is what makes it a build property instead of six independent habits.",
    "raw_day_disposal": "deletion is the DEFAULT, not the wired-in action, and the key that turns it off is declared rather than implied. Three reasons, and the first is about proof rather than disk. An input deleted before the artifact is written cannot be hashed, so a node that walks the tape can never be PROVEN unchanged, only assumed -- and reproducibility with a missing input is not checked at all. Three nodes here walk the same ten days, so without a kept day one recompute batch pays three fetches for identical bytes. And the volume is still the scarce thing, which is why keeping is the setting and deleting is what happens when nobody sets it."
  },
